infographic list-column-simple-vertical-arrow
data
  title Buffer capacity: a tuning knob with a sharp edge
  desc No finite buffer survives a dead consumer — capacity only delays the block by exactly its size, and a bigger number trades visible blocking for hidden memory growth
  lists
    - label Capacity 0 — rendezvous, no slack
      desc make(chan T) has no storage: the send blocks until a receiver takes the value hand-to-hand, so it synchronizes the two goroutines and couples producer and consumer beat-for-beat. The default senior choice — backpressure is always visible.
    - label Small buffer — sized to a measured burst
      desc make(chan T, n) where n is one real burst. Absorbs scheduling jitter and decouples the two schedules while slots remain. A full buffer is a signal that the consumer is too slow — not an obstacle to bypass with a bigger number.
    - label Huge buffer, cap 1,000,000 — hidden OOM
      desc make(chan T, 1_000_000) turns visible backpressure into invisible memory growth: the producer never feels the consumer fall behind until the process OOMs. A larger capacity only delays the deadlock by exactly that many sends.
theme
  palette #14b8a6 #f59e0b #f43f5e
